The Relay Post
[trigger none]
[vignette dungeons/relay_post]

FIXME(mundane): The room is small enough that three people make it feel crowded. A wire receiver squats on a wooden desk; behind it, hanging on pegs, the day's transcriptions clipped onto a wire. Stacks of older filings line one wall. The brazier puts off a useful amount of heat for the wind howling outside. The pot of tea on it is still warm. The two arguing parties have settled themselves to opposite ends of the room and are pretending the conversation never happened.
# --- COLOR ---
# The wire receiver on the desk has a brass cylinder attached to its base, marked with a Kesharat-issue cipher seal.
# The transcriptions clipped onto the wire behind the receiver are written in a compact, standardized script with consistent spacing and marginalia in a different hand.
# The stacks of older filings along the wall are bound with twine and labeled in faded ink with dates and regional codes.
# The brazier is made of blackened iron with a cracked enamel lining, and the coals beneath the tea pot glow faintly orange.
# The tea pot on the brazier is a dented brass vessel with a chipped spout, its handle wrapped in frayed leather.
# The two chairs at opposite ends of the room are mismatched—one a plain wooden stool, the other a padded seat with a frayed cushion and a small brass nameplate reading "Ossal, Inspector."
# --- end ---
# --- FACTUAL ---
# The room is small enough that three people make it feel crowded. A wire receiver squats on a wooden desk, its brass cylinder attached to the base marked with a Kesharat-issue cipher seal. Behind it, the day's transcriptions are clipped onto a wire, each sheet written in a compact, standardized script with consistent spacing and marginalia in a different hand. Stacks of older filings line one wall, bound with twine and labeled in faded ink with dates and regional codes. The brazier is made of blackened iron with a cracked enamel lining, and the coals beneath the tea pot glow faintly orange. The tea pot on the brazier is a dented brass vessel with a chipped spout, its handle wrapped in frayed leather. At opposite ends of the room, the two chairs sit mismatched—one a plain wooden stool, the other a padded seat with a frayed cushion and a small brass nameplate reading "Ossal, Inspector." The two arguing parties have settled themselves to opposite ends of the room and are pretending the conversation never happened.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
# The chamber was small—so small that three men within it seemed to press upon one another like figures in a dream, their breaths mingling in the still, close air. A wire receiver squatted upon the wooden desk, its brass cylinder rigid and cold, its base stamped with the cipher seal of Kesharat—ancient, unyielding, and sealed with a wax that had long since darkened to the hue of dried blood. Behind it, the day's transcriptions were clipped to a taut wire, each sheet bearing the precise, unvarying script of the Bureau's standard hand, the letters aligned with mechanical regularity, the margins marked with annotations in a different hand—neat, angular, and faintly tremulous, as though written by a man whose nerves were frayed by secrets he dared not speak. Along one wall, stacks of older filings rose like ossified records, bound with frayed twine and labeled in ink that had faded to a ghostly gray—dates blurred, regional codes dissolved into insignificance. In the corner, the brazier stood—a vessel of blackened iron, its enamel lining cracked in a web of fine fissures, the coals beneath the tea pot glowing with a dull, sickly orange light, as though the fire were not of this world. The tea pot itself was a dented brass vessel, its spout chipped and jagged, its handle wrapped in leather worn thin by countless hands, frayed and brittle as old sinew. At opposite ends of the room, the two chairs sat in mute defiance: one a plain wooden stool, unadorned and hard as a judgment; the other a padded seat with a cushion long stripped of its stuffing, the stuffing now a gray dust upon the floor, and a small brass nameplate upon its back—"Ossal, Inspector"—its letters worn smooth by time and neglect. The two parties, now seated at either end, had settled into their places with a studied indifference, their eyes averted, their silence a carefully constructed lie. They pretended the conversation had never taken place—yet the air between them thrummed with unspoken words, as if something unspeakable had passed between them, something that could not be recalled, only endured.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED REH mundane ---
# The chamber was small enough that three men would make it feel choked, the air thick with the scent of old paper and faint smoke. A wire receiver sat upon the desk like a crouching beast, its brass cylinder cold and unyielding, the base stamped with the cipher seal of Kesharat—dark, precise, unblinking. Behind it, the day's transcriptions were pinned to the wire, each sheet a neat, compact cipher in standardized script, the margins marked with notes in a different hand—sharp, angular, impatient. Along one wall, stacks of older files stood like sentinels, bound with twine, their labels faded to the color of dust, dates and regional codes barely legible. The brazier was a blackened iron bowl, its enamel cracked like dried earth, the coals beneath the tea pot glowing a dull, stubborn orange. The pot itself was a dented brass vessel, its spout chipped, the handle wrapped in frayed leather that had seen too many hands. At opposite ends of the room, two chairs sat like rivals—on one side, a plain wooden stool, unadorned, unyielding; on the other, a padded seat with a cushion worn thin, the fabric frayed, its brass nameplate tarnished but still reading: "Ossal, Inspector." And there they were—two men, each at the edge of the room, backs stiff, eyes fixed on some distant point beyond the wall, pretending the argument had never been, as if silence could erase the heat of words.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC factual ---
# (no findings)
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
