        FIXME(mundane): He listens to your account of Devra's recital with patience. When you finish he responds, not with dismissal but with explanation. The Kesharat Administration is the instrument of the Lattice. The Lattice perceives connections that humans cannot. Its directives appear arbitrary because human understanding is limited to three dimensions of space and one of time. The Lattice operates across a broader topology.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use.
        # The stack of filed transcriptions on the desk is organized by date in ascending order, with a red ribbon marking the most recent batch.
        # The wire receiver on the desk is a Model-7 relay unit, its casing pitted from desert exposure and its indicator light dark.
        # The pot of tea on the brazier is the same shade as the rust-colored sky outside, its dregs thick with sediment.
        # The pencil-stub on the desk is worn to a nub, its eraser gone, and lies beside a page of notes written in Devra's cramped hand.
        # The back room door is ajar, revealing a narrow cot, a folded blanket, and a ledger open to a page with a single entry in Ossal's precise script.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # He listens to your account of Devra's recital with patience. When you finish he responds, not with dismissal but with explanation. The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use. The stack of filed transcriptions on the desk is organized by date in ascending order, with a red ribbon marking the most recent batch. The wire receiver on the desk is a Model-7 relay unit, its casing pitted from desert exposure and its indicator light dark. The pot of tea on the brazier is the same shade as the rust-colored sky outside, its dregs thick with sediment. The pencil-stub on the desk is worn to a nub, its eraser gone, and lies beside a page of notes written in Devra's cramped hand. The back room door is ajar, revealing a narrow cot, a folded blanket, and a ledger open to a page with a single entry in Ossal's precise script. The Kesharat Administration is the instrument of the Lattice. The Lattice perceives connections that humans cannot. Its directives appear arbitrary because human understanding is limited to three dimensions of space and one of time. The Lattice operates across a broader topology.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
        # He listened to your account of Devra's recital with a patience that was not merely courteous, but almost spectral in its stillness—like the hush that follows the last note of a forgotten hymn in a ruined cathedral. When you had finished, he did not dismiss your words, nor did he offer the brittle reassurance of the rationalist. Instead, he spoke with a quiet precision, as though unfolding a map drawn in a language older than speech. The brass cylinder on her desk, he explained, was a Kesharat-issue cipher seal—its surface worn smooth by countless manipulations, the grooves of its engravings worn down to a ghost of their original depth. The stack of filed transcriptions, neatly arranged in ascending order of date, bore a red ribbon at the top, marking the most recent batch—a signal, he said, not of urgency, but of vigilance. The wire receiver on the desk, a Model-7 relay unit, bore the pitted scars of desert exposure, its casing cracked like sun-baked earth, and its indicator light, though dark, had not failed—only rested. The pot of tea upon the brazier was the same dull, rust-colored hue as the sky outside, its dregs thick with sediment like the dust of forgotten epochs. The pencil-stub beside the page of notes—Devra's cramped, angular script—was worn to a nub, the eraser long gone, as if the hand that held it had scribbled its final thought in desperation. The door to the back room stood ajar, revealing a narrow cot, a blanket folded with mechanical exactness, and a ledger open to a single entry—Ossal's precise, unyielding script, each letter a blade. The Kesharat Administration, he said, was not a government, nor a bureaucracy, but the instrument of the Lattice. And the Lattice—oh, the Lattice—did not perceive the world as men do. It perceived connections, interwoven threads across dimensions not meant for human sight. Its directives seemed arbitrary only because human minds were bound to three dimensions of space and one of time—trapped in the shallow, linear flow of perception. The Lattice, by contrast, moved across a broader topology—beyond time, beyond form, beyond the very notion of distance. To it, a single moment could stretch into an eternity, a single thought
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
        # He listened to your account of Devra's recital with the patient stillness of a man who has heard worse—worse and stranger. When you finished, he did not wave it aside as idle chatter, nor did he scoff. Instead, he spoke, low and deliberate, as one who weighs every word like a blade in the hand. The brass cylinder on her desk was no mere trinket—it bore the mark of Kesharat, a cipher seal worn smooth by countless turns, its surface scarred with the fingerprints of a thousand hands. The stack of transcriptions was arranged by date, ascending, each batch tied with a red ribbon—thin as a nerve, tight as a vow. The wire receiver was a Model-7 relay, its casing pitted like the hide of a desert beast, the indicator light dark, dead as a buried star. The pot of tea on the brazier was the color of the rust-colored sky outside, thick with sediment, as if the very dregs held secrets. The pencil-stub lay beside a page of notes, its eraser long gone, the lead worn to a nub, the script cramped and urgent—Devra's hand, sharp as a knife's edge. The back room door hung ajar, revealing a narrow cot, a blanket folded with military precision, and a ledger open to a single entry—Ossal's script, clean and exact, like the edge of a guillotine. The Kesharat Administration was not a government—it was a tool, a blade in the hand of the Lattice. And the Lattice? It saw beyond the walls of space and time, beyond the three dimensions of man's narrow sight. It perceived connections—threads in the dark—where we see only chaos. Its orders seemed arbitrary because we are blind, bound by our mortal span, our limited senses. But the Lattice moves through a broader topology—through the folds of the world we cannot see, where time is not a line, and space is not a cage.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
        # [CRIT] "like the hush that follows the last note of a forgotten hymn in a ruined cathedral" — INVENTION: adds unrecorded sensory comparison and emotional register to the patient listening.
        # [CRIT] "nor did he offer the brittle reassurance of the rationalist" — INVENTION: characterizes his potential response type beyond what the RECORD states.
        # [CRIT] "as though unfolding a map drawn in a language older than speech" — INVENTION: adds metaphorical description of his manner not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "by countless manipulations, the grooves of its engravings worn down to a ghost of their original depth" — INVENTION: adds detail about engravings and their state beyond "worn smooth from repeated use."
        # [CRIT] "a signal, he said, not of urgency, but of vigilance" — INVENTION: interprets the ribbon's meaning; RECORD states only that it marks the most recent batch.
        # [CRIT] "its casing cracked like sun-baked earth" — INVENTION: adds cracking detail; RECORD says only "pitted."
        # [CRIT] "and its indicator light, though dark, had not failed—only rested" — INVENTION: adds interpretive claim about the light's dormancy state beyond "dark."
        # [CRIT] "like the dust of forgotten epochs" — INVENTION: adds metaphorical characterization of the sediment not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "as if the hand that held it had scribbled its final thought in desperation" — INVENTION: adds speculation about emotional state of writer and finality of the writing act.
        # [CRIT] "a blanket folded with mechanical exactness" — INVENTION: adds detail about the folding quality; RECORD states only "folded."
        # [CRIT] "each letter a blade" — INVENTION: adds metaphorical characterization of Ossal's script not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "was not a government, nor a bureaucracy, but" — INVENTION: adds negations and categorical claims beyond RECORD's simple identification.
        # [CRIT] "oh, the Lattice" — INVENTION: adds exclamatory interjection not in RECORD; stylistically shifts to direct address/apostrophe.
        # [CRIT] "interwoven threads across dimensions not meant for human sight" — INVENTION: adds "interwoven threads" metaphor and "not meant for human sight" claim; RECORD says only "perceives connections."
        # [CRIT] "trapped in the shallow, linear flow of perception" — INVENTION: adds "trapped," "shallow," and "linear flow" characterizations beyond RECORD's simpler dimensional constraint.
        # [CRIT] "beyond time, beyond form, beyond the very notion of distance" — INVENTION: adds "beyond form" and "beyond distance" claims; RECORD specifies only broader topology across dimensional space and time.
        # [CRIT] "To it, a single moment could stretch into an eternity, a single thought" — INVENTION: adds speculative example of Lattice perception not grounded in RECORD; text cuts off mid-sentence.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
        # [CRIT] "the patient stillness of a man who has heard worse—worse and stranger" — PC INTERIORITY: attributes internal state and history to the NPC beyond the RECORD's "patience."
        # [CRIT] "he did not wave it aside as idle chatter, nor did he scoff. Instead, he spoke, low and deliberate, as one who weighs every word like a blade in the hand" — PC INTERIORITY: narrates the NPC's manner of speech, tone, and deliberative process not stated in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "no mere trinket" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: introduces a judgment/contrast not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "scarred with the fingerprints of a thousand hands" — INVENTION: adds sensory detail (fingerprints visible on surface) not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "thin as a nerve, tight as a vow" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: metaphors that add emotional/thematic weight not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "pitted like the hide of a desert beast" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: simile adds interpretive layer not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "dead as a buried star" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: intensifies "dark" with metaphorical claim.
        # [CRIT] "as if the very dregs held secrets" — PC INTERIORITY/INVENTION: attributes symbolic meaning/hidden information not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "the script cramped and urgent" — INVENTION: "urgent" adds interpretive claim about the handwriting not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "sharp as a knife's edge" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: metaphor adds judgment not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "a blanket folded with military precision" — INVENTION: adds detail about *how* folded beyond "folded."
        # [CRIT] "like the edge of a guillotine" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds ominous metaphorical weight.
        # [CRIT] "was not a government—it was a tool, a blade in the hand of the Lattice" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD states "The Kesharat Administration is the instrument of the Lattice," not that it *is* a tool/blade of the Lattice—this recasts the relationship with editorial judgment.
        # [CRIT] "It saw beyond the walls of space and time" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD says Lattice "perceives connections" and "operates across a broader topology," not that it literally "sees beyond...space and time."
        # [CRIT] "threads in the dark" — INVENTION: adds concrete metaphor for connections not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "we are blind, bound by our mortal span, our limited senses" — PC INTERIORITY: shifts from impersonal explanation to first-person human limitation.
        # [CRIT] "where time is not a line, and space is not a cage" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds poetic claims about topology's nature not stated in RECORD.
