        FIXME(dread): He says all of this calmly, reasonably, without condescension. He believes it. Fifty years of service have made his faith structural. He explains it the way you would explain a rule of grammar to a child who has not yet learned the rule.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The brass cylinder on the desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from frequent handling.
        # The ledger on the shelf contains entries in the smooth balanced script of the Kesharat administrative standard, each page marked with a date stamp from the current year.
        # The brazier holds cold ash and one unburned scrap of paper bearing a partial message in Devra's handwriting.
        # The wire receiver is connected to a looped signal line that hums faintly, though no active transmission is registered.
        # The back room door has a narrow slot beneath it, through which a thin strip of paper has been slid and left lying on the floor.
        # The teacup on the table is cracked at the rim but still bears a faint residue of cardamom and dried tea leaves.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # The brass cylinder on the desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from frequent handling. The ledger on the shelf contains entries in the smooth balanced script of the Kesharat administrative standard, each page marked with a date stamp from the current year. The brazier holds cold ash and one unburned scrap of paper bearing a partial message in Devra's handwriting. The wire receiver is connected to a looped signal line that hums faintly, though no active transmission is registered. The back room door has a narrow slot beneath it, through which a thin strip of paper has been slid and left lying on the floor. The teacup on the table is cracked at the rim but still bears a faint residue of cardamom and dried tea leaves. He says all of this calmly, reasonably, without condescension. He believes it. Fifty years of service have made his faith structural. He explains it the way you would explain a rule of grammar to a child who has not yet learned the rule.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL dread ---
        # The brass cylinder upon the desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal—its surface worn smooth by decades of anxious fingers, its edges dull as if polished by the weight of unspoken oaths. The ledger upon the shelf, bound in cracked leather, holds entries in the precise, balanced script of the Kesharat administrative standard, each page stamped with the same date—this year, this year, this year—though the numbers seem to shift when stared at too long. The brazier, cold as a grave, cradles only ash and one ragged scrap of paper, its edges blackened, bearing a single line of Devra's hand: *"They are not in the records, but they are in the silence"*—the rest obliterated as if by fire or something worse. The wire receiver, its cord coiled like a serpent, hums with a subsonic thrum, a vibration felt more in the teeth than heard, though no signal flickers upon the dial. The back room door—its paint peeling like dead skin—has a narrow slot beneath it, through which a thin strip of paper has been slid, lying flat on the floor like a message from the void. The teacup, cracked at the rim, still holds a faint residue of cardamom and dried leaves, though it has been cold for hours. He speaks of all this with a calm so absolute it borders on the inhuman—a voice not of reason, but of doctrine. He believes it, not because he has seen, but because he has been taught to believe. Fifty years of service have forged his faith into something structural, like the bones of a building. He explains it as one would explain a rule of grammar to a child who has not yet learned the rule—patiently, logically, without irony, without fear—though his eyes, when they meet yours, are the eyes of a man who has looked too long into the dark beyond the world.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH dread ---
        # The brass cylinder on the desk was a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth as a river stone, polished by the fingers of men who had turned its dials a hundred times in the dark. The ledger on the shelf bore entries in the cold, balanced script of Kesharat's administrative hand—each page stamped with the year's mark, as though time itself were a regulated line in a ledger of dead men. The brazier held only cold ash, and in its heart, a scrap of paper—unburned, half-scorched—bearing a fragment of Devra's hand, a name, a plea, or perhaps a curse, lost in the smoke. The wire receiver hummed with a low, insistent whisper, a ghost of a signal, though no current stirred the line, no voice spoke through the wire. The back room door, unyielding, had a narrow slot beneath it, and through it, a strip of paper had been slid like a message from the grave—left there, not thrust, but *placed*, as though by a hand that knew the watcher would see. The teacup on the table, cracked at the rim, still clung to the faint scent of cardamom and the dried leaves of tea long gone cold—dregs of a ritual not yet ended. He spoke of it all with a calm so absolute it bordered on the sacred. No mockery, no pity, only the quiet certainty of a man who had seen too much to doubt. His faith was not belief—it was bone-deep, built on fifty years of silence, of doors left ajar, of messages sent and never answered. He explained it as one might explain the rules of grammar to a child—no fury, no urgency—just the cold, unassailable truth that the world was not what it seemed, and that what was not seen might yet be waiting.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL dread ---
        # [CRIT] "worn smooth by decades of anxious fingers" — INVENTION of the specific mechanism of wear.
        # [CRIT] "its edges dull as if polished by the weight of unspoken oaths" — INVENTION of a metaphysical cause and loaded claim about oaths.
        # [CRIT] "bound in cracked leather" — INVENTION of binding material and condition.
        # [CRIT] "each page stamped with the same date—this year, this year, this year—though the numbers seem to shift when stared at too long" — INVENTION of a perceptual phenomenon and contradiction (the RECORD specifies multiple date stamps, not repetition, and no shifting effect).
        # [CRIT] "cold as a grave" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing the specific quality of grave-coldness as a new claim.
        # [CRIT] "one ragged scrap of paper, its edges blackened, bearing a single line of Devra's hand: *'They are not in the records, but they are in the silence'*—the rest obliterated as if by fire or something worse" — INVENTION of the complete text of the message, edge condition, and a speculative cause of obliteration. The RECORD only says "partial message."
        # [CRIT] "The wire receiver, its cord coiled like a serpent" — INVENTION of cord appearance and coiled state.
        # [CRIT] "hums with a subsonic thrum, a vibration felt more in the teeth than heard" — INVENTION of subsonic frequency and tactile sensation. The RECORD says only "hums faintly."
        # [CRIT] "though no signal flickers upon the dial" — INVENTION of a dial and visual phenomenon.
        # [CRIT] "The back room door—its paint peeling like dead skin" — INVENTION of paint condition.
        # [CRIT] "lying flat on the floor like a message from the void" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing metaphysical claim.
        # [CRIT] "though it has been cold for hours" — INVENTION of temporal detail about when the cup cooled.
        # [CRIT] "He speaks of all this with a calm so absolute it borders on the inhuman—a voice not of reason, but of doctrine" — CONTRADICTION. The RECORD states "He says all of this calmly, reasonably" and "He believes it."
        # [CRIT] "He believes it, not because he has seen, but because he has been taught to believe" — INVENTION of the psychological mechanism underlying his belief.
        # [CRIT] "Fifty years of service have forged his faith into something structural, like the bones of a building" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT adding architectural metaphor beyond "structural."
        # [CRIT] "patiently, logically, without irony, without fear—though his eyes, when they meet yours, are the eyes of a man who has looked too long into the dark beyond the world" — INVENTION of eye contact and an interpretive claim about his gaze and interior state.
        # [CRIT] "though his eyes, when they meet yours" — PC INTERIORITY (second person "yours," narrating the player character's experience of being looked at).
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced REH dread ---
        # [CRIT] "polished by the fingers of men who had turned its dials a hundred times in the dark" — INVENTION: adds unrecorded history of handling and darkness.
        # [CRIT] "as though time itself were a regulated line in a ledger of dead men" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: introduces interpretive claim about dead men not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "a name, a plea, or perhaps a curse, lost in the smoke" — INVENTION: speculates on content of Devra's partial message beyond RECORD's "partial message."
        # [CRIT] "a ghost of a signal" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds supernatural quality not present in RECORD's factual "hums faintly."
        # [CRIT] "no current stirred the line, no voice spoke through the wire" — INVENTION: adds negations not stated in RECORD; contradicts "no active transmission is registered" by specifying "no voice."
        # [CRIT] "like a message from the grave" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: introduces death/supernatural framing absent from RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "placed, as though by a hand that knew the watcher would see" — INVENTION: adds intentionality and awareness of observation not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "dregs of a ritual not yet ended" — INVENTION: frames teacup as part of unfinished ritual, not stated in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "His faith was not belief—it was bone-deep, built on fifty years of silence, of doors left ajar, of messages sent and never answered" — PC INTERIORITY + INVENTION: narrates internal state ("not belief") and interprets his past with specific scenarios not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "the world was not what it seemed, and that what was not seen might yet be waiting" — PC INTERIORITY: attributes philosophical/existential thoughts to the speaker beyond the RECORD's description.
        # --- end ---
        +add_tag relay_post.met_ossal
    }
    +open "The Relay Post"

* Read the message file = Look at the wire's recent traffic closely [requires !tag relay_post.saw_wire || tag relay_post.met_devra && !tag relay_post.predicted_arrival]
    @if tag relay_post.saw_wire {
        FIXME(dread): With Devra's account fresh, you go back through the file. You find what she had not yet shown you: a maintenance notice from earlier this week. Routine in form. The coordinates match this relay's location. The text reads, "Confirm operational status of relay node at coordinates. Expect transient personnel." The timestamp is from before you arrived. You are the transient personnel.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface etched with the cold geometries of empire.
        # The wire receiver on the desk is cold to the touch, its connection still active despite the storm.
        # The ledger on the desk contains entries in the smooth balanced script of the plateau, each page marked with a red stamp that reads "Lattice Alignment Verified."
        # The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface crusted with a thin film of dried residue.
        # The second notebook, tucked beneath a stack of transcriptions, is bound in worn leather and contains handwritten annotations in Devra's precise hand.
        # The signal tower on the roof is silent, its brass antenna bent at a slight angle from the wind.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface etched with the cold geometries of empire. The wire receiver on the desk is cold to the touch, its connection still active despite the storm. The ledger on the desk contains entries in the smooth balanced script of the plateau, each page marked with a red stamp that reads "Lattice Alignment Verified." The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface crusted with a thin film of dried residue. The second notebook, tucked beneath a stack of transcriptions, is bound in worn leather and contains handwritten annotations in Devra's precise hand. The signal tower on the roof is silent, its brass antenna bent at a slight angle from the wind. You go back through the file. You find what she had not yet shown you: a maintenance notice from earlier this week. Routine in form. The coordinates match this relay's location. The text reads, "Confirm operational status of relay node at coordinates. Expect transient personnel." The timestamp is from before you arrived. You are the transient personnel.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL dread ---
        # The brass cylinder upon her desk was no mere cipher seal—it was a Kesharat-issue node, its surface etched with the cold, angular geometries of an empire long dead, yet still breathing in the dark. The wire receiver lay cold, its metal casing unyielding to the touch, though the line remained live, humming faintly with a presence that was not of this world. The ledger, bound in supple hide from some long-extinct beast, bore entries in the smooth, balanced script of the plateau, each page stamped with a crimson sigil: "Lattice Alignment Verified." And yet, beneath that seal, something older stirred—something that should not have been written, and certainly should not have been confirmed. The pot of tea upon the brazier was cold, its surface cracked with a thin, crystalline film, as if the liquid had dried not in time, but in the manner of something that had never truly been warm. Beneath a stack of transcriptions, the second notebook lay hidden—bound in worn, grey leather, its pages brittle with age, its annotations in Devra's precise, meticulous hand, each line a careful cipher against the void. The signal tower on the roof stood silent, its brass antenna bent at a subtle, unnatural angle, as though twisted by a force not of wind, but of will. You turned back through the file—slowly, with the dread of one who knows he is being read—and found what she had not yet shown you: a maintenance notice, typed in the sterile, routine cadence of the Archive. "Confirm operational status of relay node at coordinates. Expect transient personnel." The coordinates matched this very relay. The timestamp was from before you arrived. And you—yes, you—were the transient personnel. The realization came not as a thought, but as a shudder that passed through the marrow, for you had not arrived. You had always been here. You had only just remembered.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH dread ---
        # The brass cylinder upon her desk was no mere cipher seal—it was a Kesharat-issue ward, its surface carved with the cold, unyielding geometries of a dead empire, each line a rune of binding, each angle a trap for the unwary. The wire receiver lay cold as a corpse's hand, yet its connection pulsed faintly, a whisper of life in the dead wire. The ledger, bound in stiff leather, bore the smooth, balanced script of the plateau, each page stamped with a red seal that read, *Lattice Alignment Verified*—a lie, for the alignment was broken, and the silence from the sky was not silence at all, but the breath before the scream.
        #
        # The pot of tea on the brazier was cold, its surface cracked with a film of dried residue, like the skin of a corpse left too long in the sun. Beneath the transcriptions, the second notebook—worn, bound in cracked leather—held Devra's precise hand, the words sharp, urgent, trembling with a fear she had not dared to voice. And the signal tower on the roof… silent. Its brass antenna bent at a sickly angle, as though something had struck it from above, or pulled it down.
        #
        # You turned back through the files—too late, always too late. There, hidden in the margins, a maintenance notice, routine in tone, but with a cold weight beneath the words: *Confirm operational status of relay node at coordinates. Expect transient personnel.* The coordinates matched this very relay. The timestamp: before you arrived. Before you had ever stepped foot in this place.
        #
        # And then it came—the truth, sharp and black as a tombstone.
        #
        # You were the transient.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL dread ---
        # [CRIT] "no mere cipher seal—it was a Kesharat-issue node" — CONTRADICTION. The RECORD identifies the object as a "cipher seal," not a "node."
        # [CRIT] "an empire long dead, yet still breathing in the dark" — INVENTION. The RECORD does not characterize the empire as dead or attribute agency to it.
        # [CRIT] "humming faintly with a presence that was not of this world" — INVENTION. The RECORD states the connection is "still active" but does not describe humming or non-worldly presence.
        # [CRIT] "bound in supple hide from some long-extinct beast" — INVENTION. The RECORD does not describe the ledger's binding material or origin.
        # [CRIT] "And yet, beneath that seal, something older stirred—something that should not have been written, and certainly should not have been confirmed" — INVENTION. The RECORD contains no such implication of forbidden content.
        # [CRIT] "as if the liquid had dried not in time, but in the manner of something that had never truly been warm" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD simply states the tea is "cold" with "dried residue"; this adds a metaphysical claim about the nature of the drying.
        # [CRIT] "bound in worn, grey leather, its pages brittle with age" — INVENTION. The RECORD specifies "worn leather" but does not characterize the color, brittleness, or age of the pages.
        # [CRIT] "each line a careful cipher against the void" — INVENTION. The RECORD describes annotations but does not characterize them as ciphers or suggest they resist the void.
        # [CRIT] "bent at a subtle, unnatural angle, as though twisted by a force not of wind, but of will" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD states the antenna is "bent at a slight angle from the wind"; attributing this to "will" rather than wind is a contradiction.
        # [CRIT] "slowly, with the dread of one who knows he is being read" — PC INTERIORITY. Narrates the PC's emotional state and metacognitive knowledge.
        # [CRIT] "typed in the sterile, routine cadence of the Archive" — INVENTION. The RECORD does not specify the medium (typed vs. handwritten) or attribute it to "the Archive."
        # [CRIT] "The realization came not as a thought, but as a shudder that passed through the marrow, for you had not arrived. You had always been here. You had only just remembered." — PC INTERIORITY and INVENTION. Narrates the PC's internal experience, metaphysical state, and memory, none of which appear in the RECORD.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced REH dread ---
        # [CRIT] "it was a Kesharat-issue ward" — contradicts RECORD's "cipher seal"; these are different object types.
        # [CRIT] "each line a rune of binding, each angle a trap for the unwary" — INVENTION: no runic or trap properties stated in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "its connection pulsed faintly" — INVENTION: RECORD states connection is "active" but adds no pulsing property.
        # [CRIT] "a whisper of life in the dead wire" — INVENTION: no characterization of the wire's state as "dead" in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "bound in stiff leather" — INVENTION: RECORD does not describe ledger binding material.
        # [CRIT] "a lie, for the alignment was broken" — INVENTION: RECORD contains no claim that the stamp's contents are false or that alignment is broken.
        # [CRIT] "and the silence from the sky was not silence at all, but the breath before the scream" — INVENTION: no such metaphysical property or threat stated in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "like the skin of a corpse left too long in the sun" — INVENTION: loaded embellishment introducing corpse imagery not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "the words sharp, urgent, trembling with a fear she had not dared to voice" — PC INTERIORITY and INVENTION: RECORD states annotations are in "precise hand" but adds no emotional content, urgency, or fear.
        # [CRIT] "as though something had struck it from above, or pulled it down" — INVENTION: RECORD states bend is "from the wind"; this introduces external agency not supported.
        # [CRIT] "You turned back through the files—too late, always too late" — PC INTERIORITY: temporal/emotional framing of the PC's action and regret not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "hidden in the margins" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD states notice was found in "the file," not "margins."
        # [CRIT] "routine in tone, but with a cold weight beneath the words" — INVENTION: RECORD states it is "routine in form" but adds no ominous undertone.
        # [CRIT] "Before you had ever stepped foot in this place" — PC INTERIORITY: adds a memory/backstory detail not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "And then it came—the truth, sharp and black as a tombstone" — PC INTERIORITY: dramatizes the PC's moment of realization with invented emotional/sensory content.
