        # --- end ---

        FIXME(dread): You check the header. There is no header. The message did not come from the capital. The message did not come from anywhere. It arrived through the wire and was filed.
        +add_tag relay_post.predicted_arrival
    } @else {
        FIXME(mundane): The file is a few weeks of standard wire traffic with the strange orders interleaved. Most of it is mundane: patrol schedules, medical requests, census updates. The strange ones stand out by their flatness. A resonance platform. Aligned calcium. An employee with a malformed ID. A facility that does not exist. Each message is in the same neat hand, filed by date.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The file contains a stack of transcribed messages bound with a frayed leather thong, the edges worn smooth from repeated handling.
        # Each message is written in the same neat, precise hand using indigo ink that has bled slightly at the edges of some pages.
        # The file includes a separate notebook with handwritten annotations in red ink, detailing discrepancies between the messages and known locations, materials, and personnel records.
        # One page bears a smudge of tea near the corner, likely from the pot on the brazier during a late-night session.
        # The file is stored in a metal box with a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, though the seal is cracked and does not appear to have been resealed.
        # A single message is folded and tucked beneath the main stack, its corner slightly torn, as if removed and returned hastily.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # The file lies open on the desk, a stack of transcribed messages bound with a frayed leather thong, the edges worn smooth from repeated handling. Each message is written in the same neat, precise hand using indigo ink that has bled slightly at the edges of some pages. Among them, a separate notebook is tucked beneath the main stack, its pages filled with handwritten annotations in red ink, detailing discrepancies between the messages and known locations, materials, and personnel records. One page bears a smudge of tea near the corner, likely from the pot on the brazier during a late-night session. The file is stored in a metal box with a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, though the seal is cracked and does not appear to have been resealed. A single message is folded and tucked beneath the main stack, its corner slightly torn, as if removed and returned hastily. Most of the messages are mundane: patrol schedules, medical requests, census updates. The strange ones stand out by their flatness—resonance platform, aligned calcium, an employee with a malformed ID, a facility that does not exist—each filed by date in the same precise hand.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
        # The file lay open upon the desk, its contents preserved beneath a frayed leather thong whose edges were worn smooth by the ceaseless, nervous handling of anxious hands. The transcribed messages were bound in a sequence both methodical and unyielding, each inscribed in the same precise, unerring hand—neat, angular, and cold as a cipher. The indigo ink had bled slightly at the corners of certain pages, as though the writer had pressed too hard, or as though time itself had seeped through the paper and blurred the boundaries between word and memory. Beneath the main stack, a separate notebook lay half-hidden, its pages thick with annotations in red ink—sharp, urgent, and scrawling, as if the author had written them in haste, driven by a dread too deep for calm. The discrepancies were numerous: dates that did not align, locations that had no place on any map, materials listed that defied known chemistry, personnel records that could not be verified. One page bore a faint, dark smudge near the corner—a tea stain, perhaps, from the cracked pot on the brazier, long since cold, where the writer had sat too long into the night, tracing connections that should not have been there. The entire file was housed within a metal box, its surface dull and dented, bearing a Kesharat-issue cipher seal—now cracked, the wax split and unsealed, as though the seal had been broken and not replaced. And beneath the main stack, a single message, folded with care, its corner torn as though hastily withdrawn and returned, its contents untouched by time yet heavy with unspoken weight. The majority of the messages were routine—patrol schedules, medical requisitions, census updates—banal, ordinary, and therefore more terrible for their ordinariness. But among them stood the others, flat and unadorned, like stones dropped into a still pool: a resonance platform, calcium aligned in impossible patterns, an employee with a malformed ID, a facility that did not exist. Each filed by date, in that same meticulous hand—so precise, so calm, so utterly without fear.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
        # The file lay open upon the desk, a stack of transcribed messages bound with a frayed leather thong, the edges worn smooth as bone from countless fingers turning the pages. Each message bore the same neat, precise hand, the ink a deep indigo that had bled slightly at the corners of some sheets, like blood seeping through parchment. Beneath the main stack, a small notebook lay tucked, its pages filled with red annotations—sharp, urgent, like wounds—detailing discrepancies between the messages and the known records: locations that didn't match, materials that couldn't be accounted for, personnel whose names rang false. One page bore the ghost of a tea stain near the corner, a dark, ringed smear from the pot on the brazier, left behind during a long night of vigil. The whole file was sealed within a metal box, its cipher lock stamped with the mark of Kesharat, yet the seal was cracked, the wax split and never resealed. A single message, folded and tucked beneath the stack, bore a torn corner—snagged, perhaps, then hastily returned. Most of the messages were dull as dust: patrol rotations, medical requisitions, census tallies. But the strange ones stood out—flat, lifeless, yet thrumming with the quiet dread of the impossible: *resonance platform*, *aligned calcium*, *employee with malformed ID*, *facility that does not exist*. All filed in order, by date, in that same precise, unyielding hand.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
        # [CRIT] "whose edges were worn smooth by the ceaseless, nervous handling of anxious hands" — PC INTERIORITY: attributes emotional state ("nervous," "anxious") to the handling, narrating unobserved mental condition.
        # [CRIT] "cold as a cipher" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: introduces a temperature/sensory quality not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "as though the writer had pressed too hard, or as though time itself had seeped through the paper and blurred the boundaries between word and memory" — INVENTION: speculates on cause of bleeding and adds metaphysical claim ("time itself") beyond RECORD's neutral observation.
        # [CRIT] "sharp, urgent, and scrawling, as if the author had written them in haste, driven by a dread too deep for calm" — PC INTERIORITY & INVENTION: narrates the author's emotional state ("dread") and urgency not specified in RECORD; RECORD only notes they are "handwritten annotations."
        # [CRIT] "dates that did not align, locations that had no place on any map, materials listed that defied known chemistry, personnel records that could not be verified" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD lists discrepancies "between the messages and known locations, materials, and personnel records"—it does not claim locations have "no place on any map" or materials "defy known chemistry." This rewrites the nature of the discrepancies.
        # [CRIT] "a tea stain, perhaps, from the cracked pot on the brazier, long since cold, where the writer had sat too long into the night, tracing connections that should not have been there" — INVENTION: RECORD says "likely from the pot on the brazier during a late-night session" but does not specify the pot is "cracked," the brazier is "long since cold," or that the writer was "tracing connections that should not have been there."
        # [CRIT] "its surface dull and dented" — INVENTION: RECORD describes the box but does not mention dents or dullness.
        # [CRIT] "its contents untouched by time yet heavy with unspoken weight" — PC INTERIORITY: "unspoken weight" projects interpretive/emotional meaning onto the message.
        # [CRIT] "banal, ordinary, and therefore more terrible for their ordinariness" — PC INTERIORITY: "more terrible" narrates an evaluative, emotional judgment not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "like stones dropped into a still pool" — INVENTION: introduces a new metaphorical image/sensory scenario not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "so precise, so calm, so utterly without fear" — INVENTION & LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: "calm" and "without fear" are unspecified emotional/psychological attributes added to the hand/writer.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
        # [LOW] "worn smooth as bone from countless fingers turning the pages" — invents the specific image of fingers turning pages and the simile to bone; RECORD only says "worn smooth from repeated handling."
        # [LOW] "like blood seeping through parchment" — invents a simile that adds a sensory/emotional claim beyond "bled slightly."
        # [LOW] "sharp, urgent, like wounds" — invents characterization of the annotations' quality and simile; RECORD only describes them as "detailing discrepancies."
        # [LOW] "a dark, ringed smear" — invents the specific visual detail "dark" and "ringed"; RECORD says only "a smudge of tea."
        # [LOW] "left behind during a long night of vigil" — invents the PC's or observer's interpretation of the session as a "vigil"; RECORD says only "late-night session."
        # [LOW] "its cipher lock stamped with the mark of Kesharat" — invents "cipher lock" and "stamped with the mark"; RECORD says only "Kesharat-issue cipher seal."
        # [LOW] "the wax split and never resealed" — invents "wax"; RECORD says only the seal is "cracked and does not appear to have been resealed."
        # [LOW] "Snagged, perhaps, then hastily returned" — invents the speculative narrative "snagged, perhaps"; RECORD says only "removed and returned hastily."
